    [38;5;153mnow[39m moves behind timestamps already in the deque. [38;5;153mcutoff[39m drops below them, nothing gets pruned, and the limiter
    stays saturated — legitimate traffic gets rejected for up to the size of the jump.
  - [1mForward jump[0m: [38;5;153mcutoff[39m leaps past every stored event, the deque empties at once, and a caller gets a full fresh burst
    of [38;5;153mlimit[39m events immediately after having just spent its budget.

  [38;5;153mtime.monotonic()[39m has neither failure mode, which is why it's the standard choice for interval measurement.

  The underlying need — correlating limiter decisions with wall-clock log timestamps — is real, but it doesn't require
  changing the window clock. Two options that keep monotonic as the source of truth:

  - Record a wall-clock timestamp [3malongside[0m the monotonic one when logging a decision ([38;5;153mtime.time()[39m at log-emit time), so
    the log line carries the wall-clock value and the window math stays monotonic.
  - Store [38;5;153m(monotonic, wall)[39m pairs in the deque if the correlation needs to be per-event rather than per-log-line.

  Tell me which you want and I'll implement it. What I won't do without you overruling me is swap the window clock
  itself.

  [1m3. Pluggable storage backend — pushing back on YAGNI grounds.[0m There's no Redis anywhere in this repo (grepped: the
  only [38;5;153mbackend[39m hit is [38;5;153mbuild-backend = "hatchling.build"[39m in [38;5;153mpyproject.toml[39m), no second limiter implementation, and no
  configuration surface that would select one. The module is 26 lines with a single call site concept.

  An interface designed now, against zero real backends, would be guessing at the hard parts: Redis can't do the
  read-prune-append sequence atomically from the client side (it needs a Lua script or a [38;5;153mMULTI[39m/[38;5;153mWATCH[39m loop), and a naive
  [38;5;153mBackend.get_events()/set_events()[39m abstraction would encode a non-atomic, racy contract that the Redis implementation
  then has to violate. The abstraction would need rewriting anyway at the point it's actually used — which is the cost
  it was supposed to avoid.

  Concrete counter-proposal: leave it as is until there's a real second backend. When Redis lands, the extraction is
  mechanical (deque operations are confined to [38;5;153mallow()[39m) and the interface can be shaped by what Redis actually needs. If
  you know Redis is definitely coming and want the seam now, say so and I'll build it — but I'd want to write the Redis
  implementation in the same change so the interface is validated against two backends instead of one.

  Net: item 1 applied and verified, items 2 and 3 awaiting your call.

